<section class="diamond-comparison"><div class="editorial-heading"><h2>Two origins. One unmistakable brilliance.</h2></div><div class="comparison-table"><div class="comparison-row comparison-head"><span></span><strong>Natural</strong><strong>Lab-grown</strong></div><div class="comparison-row"><span>Origin</span><span>Formed by nature over billions of years</span><span>Grown in controlled conditions over several weeks</span></div><div class="comparison-row"><span>Appearance</span><span>Diamond with natural characteristics</span><span>Chemically and optically identical diamond</span></div><div class="comparison-row"><span>Certification</span><span>Independent certificate for selected stones</span><span>Independent certificate marked Lab-grown</span></div><div class="comparison-row"><span>Price</span><span>Higher due to rarity and mining</span><span>More accessible at equivalent specifications</span></div></div></section>
<section class="diamond-faq"><div><p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p><h2>{{ six_faq_title }}</h2></div><div><details open><summary>Is a lab-grown diamond fake?</summary><p>No. It has the same crystal structure and physical properties. Cubic zirconia and moissanite are different materials.</p></details><details><summary>Can you tell by looking?</summary><p>No. Origin is reliably identified by a gemological laboratory using specialist equipment.</p></details><details><summary>Which offers better value?</summary><p>A lab-grown stone usually allows greater carat weight or specifications within the same budget. Natural diamonds are valued for rarity and natural origin.</p></details></div></section>
